This repository contains 45 task-scoped Agent Skills (`frappe-*`), with
`frappe-router` as the entry point.

The lifecycle skills (requirements, spec, constraints, planning, incremental
implementation, doubt-driven development, context engineering, debugging,
browser testing, review, simplification, security, performance, git workflow,
CI/CD, deprecation/migration, documentation, observability, shipping), the
Common Rationalizations / Red Flags section pattern, the slash commands, the
specialist agent personas, the validation scripts and the routing evals are
adapted from Addy Osmani's agent-skills at
https://github.com/addyosmani/agent-skills, licensed MIT (full text in
LICENSES/agent-skills-addyosmani-MIT.txt). Adapted 2026-10-24 at upstream commit
aeac41d. Each adapted skill and reference re-derives the process for Frappe and
carries an inline `Adapted from addyosmani/agent-skills` provenance note naming
the upstream file.

Portions of the routing guidance and the terse backend/frontend reference
material are derived from the upstream Frappe skills published by Frappe
Technologies at https://github.com/frappe/skills — specifically its
`frappe-app-dev` skill (global rules, flow selection, and the initial
references for doctypes, controllers, api, database, caching, realtime,
background-jobs, hooks, permissions, testing, frontend and bench operations).
That material has since been extended by source-verified references written
against installed Frappe v16, ERPNext v16 and HRMS v16 source.

Portions are adopted from the Frappe Skills published by LUBUS at
https://github.com/lubusIN/frappe-skills, licensed MIT (full text in
LICENSES/frappe-skills-lubusIN-MIT.txt). Adopted 2026-09-23 at upstream commit
afd9ea1: the whole of its 14 skills — api-development, app-development,
desk-customization, doctype-development, enterprise-patterns, frappe-manager,
frontend-development, printing-templates, project-triage, reports, router,
testing, ui-patterns, web-forms. On 2026-09-23 this repository also adopted its
structure: the router + task-skill layout, per-skill `references/`, and the
nine-section SKILL.md format (When to use, Inputs required, Procedure,
Verification, Failure modes / debugging, Escalation, References, Guardrails,
Common Mistakes). Skill names carry a `frappe-` prefix here because they install
into a shared skill root. Blocks taken from it carry an inline
`Adopted from lubusIN/frappe-skills` provenance note naming the source file.

The frappe-ui references (frontend development, UI patterns, design tokens) are
verified against frappe-ui 1.0.0-beta.29 source and re-verified against
1.0.2 (https://github.com/frappe/frappe-ui, MIT) with 0.1.x differences
noted. Parts are adapted from that repository's `skills/frappe-ui/` agent
skill, `PHILOSOPHY.md`, `CONTEXT.md`, `evals/` and `spec/` documents;
files that do so say so in their first lines.

Several review practices are adapted from gstack by Garry Tan at
https://github.com/garrytan/gstack, licensed MIT (full text in
LICENSES/gstack-garrytan-MIT.txt). Adapted 2026-10-25: confidence-scored review
findings and the pre-emit verification gate, scope-drift detection, the
completion-status protocol, specialist review passes, QA tiers with a health
score and regression baseline, post-deploy verification, the bench safety hook
(from its careful/freeze/guard hooks), plan review (office-hours,
plan-ceo-review, plan-eng-review, plan-devex-review, autoplan) and design
review (plan-design-review, design-shotgun, design-html, design-review,
ios-design-review, ios-qa). All text is rewritten for Frappe/ERPNext; no
gstack prose or code is vendored, and none of its Apache-2.0-derived design
material is used.

The bench safety hook's pipe-to-shell, secret-exfiltration, untrusted-install and
credential-in-write checks, the independent refutation pass for review findings,
the followups/truncated fields of the completion status, and the test-first
repair guardrails are adapted from ruflo (npm claude-flow) by ruvnet at
https://github.com/ruvnet/claude-flow, licensed MIT (full text in
LICENSES/ruflo-ruvnet-MIT.txt). Adapted 2026-10-25. The text and regexes are
rewritten for Frappe/bench; no ruflo code is vendored.

The spec comprehension check, the plan readiness check, the alternatives-considered
rule, the reuse search, and the spec-drift script (frappe-spec-driven-development,
frappe-planning-and-task-breakdown, frappe-plan-review) are adapted from the
Elephant-Goldfish Model by Google at
https://github.com/google/elephant-goldfish-model, licensed Apache-2.0 (full text in
LICENSES/egm-google-Apache-2.0.txt). Adapted 2026-10-27. The ideas are rewritten for
Frappe in our own words and the script is original code; no EGM text or code is
copied. This is not a Google product and Google does not endorse it.

